WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.719
Elements exist in three different phases:
solid, liquid, and gas.

00:00:08.719 --> 00:00:12.969
The boundaries of these phases are best illustrated
using phase diagrams.

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:17.660
Phase diagrams allow us to predict the element’s
phase based on the temperature and pressure

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.400
of its environment.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.750
The dividing lines are indicative of physical
processes such as melting and boiling.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:29.190
At the boundary the element experiences a
fusion of the phases (Imagine an ice cube

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.540
melting or water boiling).

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.710
On the phase diagram, there exist two interesting
points: the triple point and the critical

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:38.710
point.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.040
The triple point, the intersection of all
three phases, is the temperature and pressure

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:49.500
at which the solid, liquid and gaseous forms
of a pure substance coexist in equilibrium.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.940
The particles of the substance are loosely
arranged between the rigidity of a solid and

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.449
the free movement of a gas.

00:00:56.449 --> 00:01:01.710
The critical point is the end point of phase
equilibrium curve between liquid and gas.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:06.520
Beyond this point, they’re indistinguishable
and form a supercritical fluid, which is a

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:12.270
substance that behaves like a gas but still
dissolves substances like liquids can.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.971
Supercritical fluids are useful because they
allow us to extract one substance from another.

00:01:15.971 --> 00:01:22.360
In many ways, a supercritical fluid’s function
can be similar to that of water, a liquid

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.360
solvent.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.540
For example, to extract an object’s salt
coating, we can soak the object in water to

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.700
dissolve the salt, then boil the water to
leave the salt behind.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.790
This works because salt and water evaporate
at different temperatures and pressures.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:42.040
In the case of supercritical fluids, we can
dissolve a substance, then lower the pressure

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.360
to turn supercritical fluids into gas.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.640
This causes previously dissolved particles
of the substance to fall down for easy collection.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.910
Compared to regular liquids, supercritical
fluids have the advantage of flowing more

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:56.430
easily.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:01.700
This lets them quickly spread dissolved substances
to make space for more, meaning they dissolve

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.630
things faster.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:08.050
Because gas-like supercritical fluids are
unaffected by surface tension, they can also

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.500
get to spots that regular fluids can’t get
to.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.390
One application of supercritical fluids is
the decaffeination of coffee.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:22.739
Previous decaffeination processes used dichloromethane,
a chemical known to cause cancer, so coffee

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:27.239
beans required treatment after decaffeination
to ensure their safety.

00:02:27.239 --> 00:02:32.269
These processes also removed oils from the
beans, ruining their taste and texture.

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:36.150
Nowadays, we use supercritical CO2 instead.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:41.690
The CO2 passes over coffee beans, dissolves
their caffeine, then releases the caffeine

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:45.129
once the pressure drops and the CO2 becomes
a gas.

00:02:45.129 --> 00:02:50.459
The beans’ oil is left unchanged and up
to 95% of the caffeine is reclaimed for use

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:54.609
in other products, like energy drinks or caffeine
pills.

00:02:54.609 --> 00:03:01.209
Among other uses, supercritical fluids can
extract proteins like diterpenes (antioxidants)

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:04.420
and tetraterpenes (carotenes).

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:09.329
Supercritical CO2 can also be used to extract
vitamin E from fruits and vegetables, or even

00:03:09.329 --> 00:03:13.040
to isolate floral fragrance from flowers.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.840
Near the critical point, substances rapidly
fluctuate between liquid-like and vapor-like

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.790
volumes, resulting in a cloudy appearance.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:25.969
This phenomenon, called critical opalescence,
has fascinated scientists for centuries and

00:03:25.969 --> 00:03:31.790
been observed in a series of substances, most
popularly CO2.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.910
Scientists at Brown University recently observed
the critical opalescence in liquid helium

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:40.189
for the first time, suggesting that this phenomenon
is universal.

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:43.209
Here’s a video of their experiment.

00:03:43.209 --> 00:03:48.890
We heat the liquid helium at about 4.2 K,
and we can see the liquid start boiling.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:54.409
After about 5 minutes, the liquid level gradually
disappears and the substance enters its supercritical

00:03:54.409 --> 00:03:55.409
phase.

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:59.239
Then we stop heating the liquid when the temperature
reaches 5.4 K.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:03.799
The liquid temperature lowers and as it approaches
the critical point, critical opalescence occurs

00:04:03.799 --> 00:04:06.249
at about 5.23 K.

00:04:06.249 --> 00:04:09.010
The fluid becomes cloudy because of the density
fluctuation.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:14.170
As the temperature drops further, the liquid
level re-appears indicating the phase separation

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.090
between liquid and vapor.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:21.449
This video would make Albert Einstein very
happy, since it helps prove the theory he

00:04:21.449 --> 00:04:24.789
proposed in his paper on the theory of opalescence.

